FineWine: MetalFX as a command, for DLSS on Direct3D 12 and Vulkan; reported GPU ids

DLSS is mapped to MetalFX. For Direct3D 11 and earlier that is DXMT's business. For Direct3D
12 (Wine's vkd3d) and Vulkan programs the work has to be recorded in the program's own
command buffer, which is MoltenVK's.

- MVKCmdMetalFX records a MetalFX temporal upscale (DLSS super resolution) or frame
  interpolation (DLSS frame generation) at its place in a command buffer. Scalers and
  interpolators are kept per device and per feature; the interpolator's previous frame is a
  copy MoltenVK keeps. If the output image was not created with the usage MetalFX needs, the
  result goes through a texture of MoltenVK's own. Without MetalFX the frame is copied across.
- The request arrives through vkCmdPushConstants() with a null layout and a marker as its
  stage flags. Wine's Vulkan layer only passes through functions Vulkan defines, and this one
  reaches MoltenVK from nvngx.dll and from vkd3d without any extension being enabled.
- MetalFX is loaded at run time, so the Xcode project does not change.
- FINEWINE_GPU_VENDOR_ID and FINEWINE_GPU_DEVICE_ID (hexadecimal) and FINEWINE_GPU_NAME
  replace the ids and the name reported in VkPhysicalDeviceProperties. MoltenVK's own
  decisions keep using the real ids.

